  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Project in Kenya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kenya is a demographically young country - 80% of the population is below 35 years. The youth (18-35) constitute over 36% of the population. This significant segment of the population demonstrated renewed agency in the 2024/2025 GenZ protests, but which seems to be waning as indicated by the very low voter registration data following the Mass Voter Registration drive; extremely low youth turnout in the November 2025 by-elections and the sentiments they express that they are disengaging because of low trust in institutions of governance. On the flip side, institutions including political parties and the IEBC seem unable or willing to innovatively engage and include the youth in their processes; in rolling out the mass voter registration drive, IEBC failed to use innovative outreach and communication strategies which resonate with the youth. This will be a crucial lesson learnt in the voter drive towards the 2027 general elections.
 
Democratic resilience cannot be built through isolated interventions but should be inclusive and consultative. Similarly, empowering young people without strengthening institutions risks generating frustration and disengagement; strengthening institutions without creating credible pathways for youth participation risks irrelevance and loss of legitimacy: these two must go hand in hand. The NIMD program therefore adopts an integrated and deliberately linked approach that connects inclusive dialogue, institutional behavior change, and youth leadership development in order to strengthen Kenya&apos;s democratic governance.

This rift between young people and institutions is likely to widen in the run up to the August 2027 elections, in which the youth could decide not to participate, leading to youth disenfranchisement in these elections. But whereas being the majority, they should determine their democratic governance destiny through effective participation and inclusive political processes. This program therefore responds to a central democratic challenge in Kenya today: the growing misalignment between rising youth civic agency and the capacity of democratic institutions to engage, respond, and adapt inclusively thereby facilitating youth political participation in democratic processes.</narrative>
  </description>
